  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Upper ridging that has been over the\nsouthwest this week is now being pushed eastward as a series of \nupper troughs move inland from the west. Unseasonably warm \ntemperatures will linger today with highs a few degrees above \nnormal in the upper 50s to 60s as warm southerly flow continues to\npush into Nevada. Strong gusty southerly winds over Nevada today \nas weather gradients tighten from the oncoming weather front. \nWinds expected to reach as high as 20-30 mph with gusts up to \n35-50 mph with strongest winds over central Nevada and is expected\nto last into the early morning hours Saturday. The upper troughs \nwill also bring in periods of precipitation and mountain snow, \nstarting in western Nevada this afternoon, reaching across the \neastern portions by the evening, and lasting until Saturday \nafternoon. Snow levels will start above 8000 feet today and drop \nto 6000-6500 feet overnight, however snow is expected to stay in \nthe mountains with minor accumulations in the high passes and \nsummits as the precipitation pushes further east by the time the \ncooler air arrives. There is a 50-70% probability of high passes \nand summits above 6000 feet seeing up to a trace to 0.5 inches of\nsnow, and a 20-40% of seeing up to one inch of snow (up to 2\ninches possible for Pinto and Murray Summit), with the highest \nprobabilities along US-50 corridor. Snow is not expected to reach \nthe valleys with the exception of Ely during the overnight hours \nwhen snow levels drop around 6000 feet, with light accumulation \nexpected from a trace up to 0.3 inches.\n\nThis first weather front is expected to exit Nevada by Saturday\nafternoon, leaving calmer weather conditions that will last\nthrough Sunday with cooler temperatures near or just a few degrees\nbelow normal. By Monday, a second weather system is expected to \nimpact Nevada lasting through Tuesday with additional periods of \nprecipitation and snow. Much cooler temperatures expected with\nthe second system as highs drop a few degrees below normal Monday,\nand drop even further with some areas seeing up to 10 degrees\nbelow normal in the 30s by Tuesday. \n\nThe second weather system is expected to exit Nevada by Tuesday\nevening, however there will only be a short reprieve from the\nweather as a third system is expected by Wednesday afternoon with\nadditional periods of precipitation and snow, lasting through \nThursday evening. Temperatures will continue on a cooling trend as\nhighs expected to be up to 10-15 degrees below normal by \nThursday. \n\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence to above normal\ntemperatures today. High confidence in regards to strong gusty\nsoutherly winds today lasting through this evening. Moderate\nconfidence in regards to periods of precipitation and mountain\nsnow this evening through Saturday morning. Moderate confidence of\nsnow accumulation reaching the higher passes and summits above\n6000 feet. Moderate confidence in regards to additional weather \nsystems impacting Nevada next week. No changes to the grids at \nthis time.",
